    [
        {
            "type": "paragraph_title",
            "bbox": [
                0.114,
                0.092,
                0.275,
                0.115
            ],
            "angle": 0,
            "content": "Background"
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "bbox": [
                0.111,
                0.126,
                0.873,
                0.253
            ],
            "angle": 0,
            "content": "The capacity development (CD) project was initiated following a formal request from the South African Revenue Service (SARS) in April 2024, aiming to strengthen SARS' capacities to mobilize digital tools for intelligence and investigation. The IMF and SARS jointly identified fraud related to crypto assets as a domain of CD. This workshop, held in Pretoria in November 2025, focused specifically on customs and tax investigation, intelligence, and data analytics related to serious crimes involving crypto assets. It was funded by the State Secretariat for Economic Affairs of Switzerland (SECO). The Korean Customs Service (KCS) also supported the event by providing experts who contributed to the training.",
            "merge_prev": false
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "bbox": [
                0.111,
                0.258,
                0.884,
                0.403
            ],
            "angle": 0,
            "content": "The workshop was designed to address the growing importance of crypto assets in South Africa's economy and the associated risks for tax evasion and illicit financial flows. It provided an interactive platform for SARS staff from various divisions—including the Syndicated Tax and Customs Crime division, the Crypto-Asset Revenue Augmentation Unit, and the Data Science Team—to deepen their understanding of the crypto ecosystem, national regulatory frameworks, and practical investigation techniques. This initiative aligns with broader IMF priorities to support member countries in adapting their fiscal and customs administrations to emerging digital financial technologies, thereby enhancing revenue mobilization and compliance monitoring.",
            "merge_prev": false
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "bbox": [
                0.111,
                0.409,
                0.881,
                0.517
            ],
            "angle": 0,
            "content": "Given the rapid evolution of crypto assets and their increasing use in both legitimate and illicit activities, the workshop's objectives were to demystify blockchain technologies, share international good practices, and foster collaboration within SARS and with external partners. The technical assistance complements ongoing efforts by South African authorities to implement international standards such as the OECD Crypto-Asset Reporting Framework (CARF) and to develop regulatory clarity around crypto assets, which are classified as financial products under South African law.",
            "merge_prev": false
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph_title",
            "bbox": [
                0.113,
                0.539,
                0.39,
                0.562
            ],
            "angle": 0,
            "content": "Summary of Findings"
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "bbox": [
                0.111,
                0.573,
                0.875,
                0.717
            ],
            "angle": 0,
            "content": "South Africa's crypto asset market is relatively advanced and dynamic, with an estimated 9 to 12 percent of the population holding crypto assets and over 240 authorized crypto asset service providers operating domestically. SARS is addressing emerging challenges in both tax and customs enforcement related to crypto assets. These challenges stem from the inherent complexity of blockchain technology, the diversity of crypto asset types, and the sophisticated obfuscation techniques used by fraudsters, such as mixers, tumblers, and peer-to-peer exchanges. SARS currently prioritizes taxation of crypto assets, relying on suspicious transaction reports and indirect indicators to detect undeclared mining activities.",
            "merge_prev": false
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "bbox": [
                0.112,
                0.723,
                0.857,
                0.85
            ],
            "angle": 0,
            "content": "The workshop discussed the regulatory landscape in South Africa, where multiple agencies including the National Treasury, South African Reserve Bank, Financial Sector Conduct Authority, SARS, and the Financial Intelligence Centre collaborate through working groups to regulate crypto assets. This national landscape was compared to the French and South Korean landscapes. South Africa's approach aligns with international definitions and standards. In many countries and regions, including South Africa, the regulatory framework is evolving, with recent initiatives to implement the OECD's CARF and revise the Common Reporting Standard to improve transparency and compliance.",
            "merge_prev": false
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "bbox": [
                0.112,
                0.855,
                0.884,
                0.91
            ],
            "angle": 0,
            "content": "International experiences shared by experts from France and South Korea provided insights into effective organizational structures, investigative methodologies, and legal frameworks for crypto asset enforcement. Both French Tax administration and South Korean Customs service have developed",
            "merge_prev": false
        },
        {
            "type": "footer",
            "bbox": [
                0.116,
